# Prototype an inline quiz Joel can click

## Step

Build a throwaway `<Quiz>` / `<QuizQuestion>` that renders in an AI Coding Crash
Course lesson body, so Joel can answer a question and see what it feels like.
This step is the gate on the brief's execution authorization: build is authorized
only after Joel touches this and says it feels right.

Use the `prototype` skill. Throwaway means throwaway — this is an alignment
instrument, not the first commit of the real component.

Scope it to the smallest thing that produces a real reaction:

- Register `Quiz` and `QuizQuestion` in the lesson runtime component map
  (`apps/ai-hero/src/utils/compile-mdx.tsx:285-488`). The map already carries
  ~30 entries and already loads stateful client components dynamically
  (`compile-mdx.tsx:40-98`), so this is joining an existing pattern.
- Accept the exact shape Matt asked for, with a real object literal:
  `<QuizQuestion data={{ question, type, choices, correct, answer }} />`.
  Object props are proven to compile — `<ThemeImage urls={{ dark, light }} />`
  ships in `apps/ai-hero/content/cohort-copy.mdx:17` and the compile call sets
  `blockJS: false` (`compile-mdx.tsx:285-296`).
- Drive grading with `surveyMachine` from `@coursebuilder/survey`
  (installed at `1.0.5`), not a fresh implementation. Fresh UI, per the pinned
  decision.
- **Single-answer multiple choice only** for the prototype. Multi-select is
  unresolved; see
  [Decide multi-select handling](./decide-multi-select-in-the-inline-quiz.svx).
- **No persistence.** Answers live in component state and die on refresh. The
  answer endpoint is a separate decision and would slow the reaction down.

**Surface (Joel, 2026-07-24): a freestanding public MDX page, not a lesson.**
A standalone route in AI Hero that needs no login and renders an MDX body
containing the quiz, so Joel can open it on his phone without an account and
without touching the draft course. It ships on a Vercel preview deploy, not
production. Joel gets the link by DM when it is running.

## What Joel is being asked to react to

- Does answering feel right inline, mid-lesson, or does it interrupt reading?
- Is the correct/incorrect reveal the right amount of feedback, and does the
  authored explanation land in the right place?
- Is the `data={{ … }}` authoring shape actually pleasant to read in a body full
  of prose, or does it wreck the source?

## Resolved when

Joel has clicked through a real question in a lesson body and said either "build
it" — which fires the brief's execution authorization — or named what has to
change first.

## Result (2026-07-24) — built, awaiting Joel's reaction

The prototype exists and is deployed. The step stays **open** until Joel clicks
it, because his reaction is the whole point of the step and it is what fires the
brief's execution authorization.

**What was built.** Route `/quiz-prototype`, no auth, three files
(commit `73f6aaf` on `prototype/inline-quiz`):

- `apps/ai-hero/src/app/quiz-prototype/page.tsx` — defines the lesson content as
  an MDX **string** and runs it through `compileMDX`, the same path a real lesson
  body takes. Four questions about steering coding agents, with explanations that
  carry real weight.
- `apps/ai-hero/src/components/mdx/quiz-prototype.tsx` — lean `Quiz` /
  `QuizQuestion` UI driving `surveyMachine` from `@coursebuilder/survey@1.0.5`
  via `useMachine`, reading `state.matches('answered.correct')`. No
  `SurveyQuestion` compound components, no `offerMachine`, per the pinned call.
- `apps/ai-hero/src/utils/compile-mdx.tsx` — `Quiz` and `QuizQuestion` registered
  with `dynamic()` alongside the ~30 existing entries.

**The authoring shape works exactly as Matt asked for it.** Real object literals
inside a database-shaped MDX string compile and reach the component:

```mdx
<Quiz>
  <QuizQuestion data={{
    question: "…", type: "multiple-choice",
    choices: [{ answer: "a", label: "…" }, { answer: "b", label: "…" }],
    correct: "b",
    answer: "The explanation revealed after answering."
  }} />
</Quiz>
```

**Verification.** The worker confirmed the render at 390px width: MDX compiled,
four questions rendered, the correct choice graded, the explanation revealed.
Typecheck passed; ESLint passed with one pre-existing `<img>` warning. A full
production build reached compilation but failed at page collection because the
local database was unavailable — an environment limitation, not a code fault.
The **deployed** page is unverified: Vercel SSO protection covers all
non-custom-domain deployments and blocks automated fetches as well as browsers,
so `vercel curl` redirects to login just like a plain request.

**Deployment.** Preview at
`https://ai-hero-11aqp4o2d-skillrecordings.vercel.app/quiz-prototype`. Joel must
pass Vercel SSO to open it. The project has no protection-bypass secret and
creating one changes project settings, so it was not done unilaterally.

**Unfold finding — `correct` silently forces choice shuffling.** In
`loadQuestion`, the machine does
`question.correct || question.shuffleChoices ? shuffle(question.choices) : question.choices`
(`packages/survey/src/survey-machine.ts:57-60`). Setting an answer key implicitly
enables shuffling with no opt-out. Authored choice order is therefore never
honored on a graded question. Reasonable default for a quiz, but it is a side
effect of a field that does not advertise it, and an author who writes
"all of the above" as the last choice will be surprised. Logged as latent on the
brief.

## Reaction (2026-07-24)

Joel: "looks good to me." No changes requested to the interaction, the reveal, or
the `data={{ … }}` authoring shape.

**This fires the brief's execution authorization.** Sessions may now build the
real component, the answer endpoint, and the MDX registration. Recorded in the
brief's Notes.
